# Code Comments: Local Truth Only

## Why this rule exists

A pointer in code to a document, an ID, or a commit is a reference that rots.
IDs get archived, sections get renumbered, commits get rebased away. Once the
pointer is wrong, the comment stops being information and becomes debt — the
reader trusts it less than nothing. State the reason where it is read: in the code.

## Rule

In **source code** (any `.h` / `.hpp` / `.cpp` / `.inl` / test source; future `src/`, `Source/`, `tests/`):

**Forbidden** in comments, log messages, and test-case names:

- Feature / Slice / Bug / ADR / TechDebt IDs — `CORE-F01`, `CORE-F01-S02`, `ITEM-F03`, `TD-014`, `BUG-CORE-001`, `ADR-20261015-01`
- bare slice tags used as prefixes or tags — `S01:`, `(S08)`, `design S02`, `F12`
- design-document section numbers or handles — `§3.4`, `design §2.7`, `H8`, `layer 2`
- requirement / acceptance-criteria labels — `O1`, `I1`, `Q3`
- commit hashes
- pointers into collaboration docs — `see ACTIVE_WORK`, `as decided in the design doc`, `see TECH_DEBT`

Those identifiers belong in `docs/ai/` only.

## Style: Doxygen contracts (Chinese first, then bilingual)

- Public API uses `///`; multi-line blocks use `/** ... */`.
- Supported tags: `@brief`, `@param`, `@return`, `@warning`, and `@note` when needed.
- Internal implementation notes use plain `//`.
- **Language cadence:** formalize with **Chinese** contract comments first for author review; after approval, add **English** so each public contract has both. Log strings for maintainers follow the same cadence.
- Comment what the code cannot say by itself: why, problem solved, migration notes, invariants, preconditions, hazards, trade-offs.

## Do not annotate everything

- **No per-field or per-method commentary is required.** A getter that returns `m_Size` needs no comment.
- An obvious comment is noise: it teaches the reader to skip comments, which costs the whole file its voice. Delete it.
- If a name does not express the intent, prefer renaming over commenting.

## Counterexample → example

### 1) A conditional whose reason was only written in a design doc

```cpp
// Bad: §H8 (ii): the checkbox binds the AUTHORED flag.
// Implements the authoring rule; see CORE-F01-S02 in ACTIVE_WORK.
if (node->IsCheckbox())
    node->SetFlag(Flag::Authored);
```

Why it fails: `§H8 (ii)`, `CORE-F01-S02` and `ACTIVE_WORK` all point outside the
file. A reader without the design doc learns nothing; a reader with an old one
learns something wrong.

```cpp
// Example: the checkbox binds the authored flag, so switching an ancestor off
// never rewrites the author's own choice.
if (node->IsCheckbox())
    node->SetFlag(Flag::Authored);
```

Why it works: the invariant is stated in domain language, inside the file that
enforces it. It stays true after every renumbering.

### 2) A test name that encoded a slice instead of the behaviour

```cpp
// Bad: the test name is a registry lookup key, not a description.
TEST_CASE("CORE-F01-S02 overrides inherit") { ... }
```

Why it fails: the name says which slice wrote it, not what breaks when it fails.
Renaming or archiving the slice makes the name meaningless, and a failing run
tells nobody what behaviour regressed.

```cpp
// Example: the name states the observable behaviour that must hold.
TEST_CASE("child override keeps ancestor default intact") { ... }
```

Why it works: the name is the specification of the failure. It needs no registry
entry beside it, now or in two years.

## Allowed elsewhere

- Feature / Slice / Bug / ADR / TechDebt IDs in `docs/ai/**` (registry, ACTIVE_WORK, design, plan, progress, debt), in commit messages, and in PR text are correct and expected.
- Log strings may use short gameplay labels; prefer no IDs there either.
